\section{Organization and Notation}

This supplement carries the derivations, specifications and experiments that
the main paper states or cites but has no room to develop. Part~I gives in
full the derivations the main paper compresses to their key steps; Part~II
specifies the reference bounds, numerical settings and baseline adaptations
that the main text names only in words; Part~III reports the numerical
results that lie outside the main paper, chiefly below its declared
operating region and under model mismatch.

Abbreviations defined in the main paper retain their meanings. Throughout,
$P_{\rm cat}$ denotes the empirical position catastrophic-event rate unless
qualified otherwise, p95 the 95th
percentile, c-RMSE the inlier-conditional root-mean-square error, NMSE
normalized mean-square error, and RSS resident set size. Unqualified vector norms are Euclidean and
unqualified matrix norms are spectral.

Unless otherwise stated, each accuracy point contains $480$ Monte Carlo
trials, and the external benchmark contains $960$ trials per point. Methods
compared within one experiment use identical scene, training-code,
Jones-state, gain and noise realizations, so comparisons within an
experiment are paired; independently generated experiments share no
realization and are interpreted separately. All numerical parameters are
fixed across SNRs and trials unless they are the stated variable of an
ablation, and no estimator is tuned for this supplement.

\section*{Part I: Detailed Derivations}

\section{Observation Stacking and Factorization}
\label{sec:coords_supp}

On an $M_{Rx}\times M_{Ry}$ grid of spacings $(d_x,d_y)$ the panel elements
are centered on the panel in its own frame,
\begin{equation}
	\brho_{k,m}=\left[\left(m_x-\tfrac{M_{Rx}-1}{2}\right)d_x,\;
	\left(m_y-\tfrac{M_{Ry}-1}{2}\right)d_y,\;0\right]^{\mathsf T},
	\label{eq:s_rho}
\end{equation}
and the BS uniform linear array lies along the global $x$ axis at
$x_i=(i-\tfrac{M_A-1}{2})d_A$ for $i=0,\ldots,M_A-1$, with
$\{\bm u_x,\bm u_y,\bm u_z\}$ the global axes and $\kappa=2\pi/\lambda_c$ the
center-frequency wavenumber.

The transverse basis of main-paper Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:qbasis} is built from
$\bm u_z$, which degenerates when the RIS-to-BS direction approaches the
pole. When $|\bs_{{\rm RB},k}^{\mathsf T}\bm u_z|>0.95$, $\bm u_y$ replaces
$\bm u_z$, which keeps the cross product well conditioned. The substitution is a gauge choice and changes no
observable: any other transverse frame differs by a $2\times2$ orthogonal
factor that the unknown Jones state absorbs, so
$\bTheta_k^{(6)}$ changes only by right multiplication with that factor and
every quantity in Theorems~\ref{main-thm:split}--\ref{main-thm:clock} is
invariant.

Finally, the stacking that produces main-paper Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:Ak} is
read off one scalar sample. Pilots are unit modulus and removed by pilot
normalization, so with $y_{i,n,t}$ the sample at receive index $i$,
subcarrier $n$ and configuration $t$,
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
	y_{i,n,t}=\sum_{k=1}^{K}
	&\Big(\sum_{m=1}^{M_R}[\bOmega_k]_{t,m}[\bg_k]_m\Big)\\
	&\times e^{-j2\pi\left(n-\frac{N-1}{2}\right)\Delta f\tau_k}\,
	[\bBm_k]_{i,:}\bx_k+n_{i,n,t},
\end{split}
	\label{eq:s_scalar}
\end{equation}
with $\bg_k=\bg_k(\bp)$ as in main-paper Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:hk}. The inner
sum is the reflection at panel $k$, the $t$th entry of $\bh_k(\bp)$,
carrying both the spherical UE--RIS and plane-wave RIS--BS phases; the
exponential is the OFDM ramp of the cascaded delay; and
$[\bBm_k]_{i,:}\bx_k$ is the Maxwell--Jones response. All $K$ panels
reflect in every configuration, so their contributions add coherently, and
because \eqref{eq:s_scalar} separates in $t$, $n$ and $i$, ordering the
samples with the EVS index fastest and stacking reproduces main-paper
Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:Ak}, while reading $i$ down the rows and $n$ across the
columns at fixed $t$ gives main-paper Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:obsmat}.

\section{Spatial-Narrowband Bound}
\label{sec:squint_supp}

Section~\ref{main-subsec:defchain} of the main paper defines the neglected
spatial phase $\epsilon_{\rm sq}$ and audits it through the exact wavefront
differences. This section derives the closed-form design-time screen that
accompanies that audit.

Write $f_n=f_c(1+\beta_n)$ with $\beta_n=\nu_n\Delta f/f_c$ at the centered
tone indices, so that $|\beta_n|\le\beta_{\rm BW}/2$ with fractional
bandwidth $\beta_{\rm BW}=B_{\rm occ}/f_c$. The exact element phase at tone
$n$ is $2\pi f_n\delta_{k,m}/c_0=\kappa\delta_{k,m}
+\kappa\beta_n\delta_{k,m}$, whose first term is the center-frequency
manifold retained by the estimator. The neglected phase is therefore
\begin{equation}
	\epsilon_{{\rm sq},k}
	=\max_{n,m}\,\kappa\,|\beta_n|\,|\delta_{k,m}|
	\le\frac{\pi\beta_{\rm BW}}{\lambda_c}\max_m|\delta_{k,m}| ,
	\label{eq:squint_step}
\end{equation}
so a bound on the element path difference bounds the discarded phase.
Splitting $\delta_{k,m}$ into the linear aperture projection and the residual
curvature as in main-paper Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:fresnel}, with aperture radius
$a_k=\max_m\|\brho_{k,m}\|_2$, UE range $R_k$ and in-plane direction
component $\bu_{k,\parallel}$,
\begin{equation}
	\max_m|\delta_{k,m}|
	\le a_k\|\bu_{k,\parallel}\|_2+\frac{a_k^2}{2R_k},
	\label{eq:squint_delta}
\end{equation}
which holds for every in-panel element offset whenever $R_k>a_k$, the first
term being the plane-wave projection and the second the curvature left after
it is removed. Substituting
\eqref{eq:squint_delta} into \eqref{eq:squint_step} and dropping the $O(1)$
constants gives the screen
\begin{equation}
	\beta_{\rm BW}\left(
	\frac{a_k}{\lambda_c}\|\bu_{k,\parallel}\|_2
	+ \frac{a_k^2}{\lambda_c R_k}\right)\ll 1,
	\label{eq:squint_cond}
\end{equation}
whose second term is the fractional bandwidth times the Fresnel number
$R_{{\rm Fr},k}/R_k$ with the Fresnel-number length
$R_{{\rm Fr},k}=a_k^2/\lambda_c$, distinct from the Fraunhofer distance
$R_{\rm FF}$ of main-paper (A3). Near broadside the linear aperture term is
small and the curvature term dominates; away from broadside the conventional
squint term $\beta_{\rm BW}a_k/\lambda_c$ must also be small. The same test
applies separately to the inter-element path differences of the RIS--BS and
BS-ULA responses: knowing the infrastructure geometry does not remove the
frequency dependence of its spatial response.

Because \eqref{eq:squint_step} maximizes over $n$ and $m$ and
\eqref{eq:squint_delta} is a worst-case aperture bound,
\eqref{eq:squint_cond} is a design screen and not a substitute for the exact
audit. In the reference configuration ($\beta_{\rm BW}=0.52\%$) the audited
values are $0.102$, $0.058$ and $0.048$~rad, with maximum wavefront
differences of $31.3$, $17.8$ and $14.8$~mm.

\section{Hankel Weighting and Coupled Recovery}

Pole estimation runs on the compressed frequency matrix
$\bY_f\in\C^{N\times rT}$ of main-paper Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:freqsnap}, which
treats the compressed receive and training indices as snapshots. It is
augmented in forward--backward form
$\bY_{\rm aug}=[\bY_f,\ \bJ\bY_f^{\ast}]$ with $\bJ$ the exchange matrix.
The $K$ leading left singular vectors of $\bY_{\rm aug}$ give the signal
subspace $\bE_s$; the shift-invariant pair is $\bE_1=\bE_s(1{:}N-1,:)$ and
$\bE_2=\bE_s(2{:}N,:)$, and the rotation is taken by total least squares,
$\bPsi_{\rm TLS}=-\bV_{12}\bV_{22}^{-1}$ from the right singular vectors of
$[\bE_1,\bE_2]$, falling back to ordinary least squares when
$N-1<2K$. The poles are the eigenvalues of $\bPsi_{\rm TLS}$, each divided
by its modulus so that only the delay phase survives. The shift invariance
is therefore read on the tone index itself, over the full $N$-tone
aperture, and no smoothing window enters the pole estimate.

The smoothing weights of main-paper Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:coupledls_short} use
the balanced window $P=\lceil N/2\rceil$, $L=N+1-P$, so that
$w_n=\#\{(p,\ell):p+\ell=n\}$ over $0\le p<P$, $0\le\ell<L$ is the
triangular anti-diagonal count of the plain Hankel lift
$[\mathcal Z]_{i,p,\ell,t}=[\bY_{{\rm d},t}]_{i,\,p+\ell}$, $i=1,\ldots,r$.
Posing
\eqref{main-eq:coupledls_short} in that lifted form gives a system whose
$k$th design column
is $\bd_{P,k}\otimes\bd_{L,k}$ with $[\bd_{P,k}]_p=z_k^{p}$ and
$[\bd_{L,k}]_\ell=z_k^{\ell}$, matching main-paper
Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:dk} up to the column-wise phase
$e^{j\pi(N-1)\Delta f\tau_k}$ that the factor coefficients absorb. Since
$\sum_{p,\ell}z_k^{p+\ell}\overline{z_l^{p+\ell}}
=\sum_n w_n z_k^n\bar z_l^n$, and identically for the correlation against
the data, the lifted normal equations and the weighted matrix ones of
\eqref{main-eq:coupledls_short} are the same linear system. Nothing is
decomposed in the lifted domain.

\section{Local Identifiability with Unknown Jones Nuisance}
\label{sec:ident_proof_supp}

Let $(\bchi_\star,\bx_\star)$ be the true parameters, let
$\bD_\chi=[\partial(\bA\bx_\star)/\partial\chi_i]_{i=1}^4$, and define
\begin{equation}
 \widetilde{\bD}_\chi=
 \begin{bmatrix}
 \Real\{\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\}\\
 \Imag\{\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\}
 \end{bmatrix},\qquad
 \bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}=\bI-\bA_\star\bA_\star^\dagger.
 \label{eq:s_ident}
\end{equation}

\begin{proposition}[Local position--clock identifiability]
Suppose $\bA_\star$ has constant rank near the truth, the paths are
resolvable in the raw OFDM domain, the panel assignment is fixed, the truth
is interior, and $\rank_{\R}(\widetilde{\bD}_\chi)=4$. Then $\bchi_\star$
is first-order locally identifiable after eliminating the complex Jones
coefficients. Under white Gaussian noise this rank condition is equivalent
to positive definiteness of the free-Jones EFIM.
\end{proposition}

\begin{IEEEproof}
A first-order perturbation is
$\delta\bmu=\bD_\chi\,\delta\bchi+\bA_\star\delta\bx$ with
$\delta\bchi\in\R^4$. A nonzero
geometric direction can be canceled by a Jones perturbation exactly when
$\bD_\chi\,\delta\bchi\in\coll(\bA_\star)$, or equivalently
$\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\,\delta\bchi=\mathbf0$. Realifying this map gives
\eqref{eq:s_ident}, whose nullspace is trivial precisely at real rank four.
The equivalence with the free-Jones EFIM follows from the elimination below.
\end{IEEEproof}

The main paper states the eliminated information in closed form; the
elimination itself is the following. Collect the real parameters as
$\bvt=[\bchi^{\mathsf T},\Real\{\bx\}^{\mathsf T},
\Imag\{\bx\}^{\mathsf T}]^{\mathsf T}\in\R^{4+4K}$. For
$\by\sim\mathcal{CN}(\bmu(\bvt),\sigma^2\bI)$ with known $\sigma^2$ the
Fisher information is
$\bJ(\bvt)=\frac{2}{\sigma^2}\Real\{\bG^{\mathsf H}\bG\}$ with
$\bG=\partial\bmu/\partial\bvt^{\mathsf T}$. Since
$\bmu=\bA(\bchi)\bx$ is linear in $\bx$,
\begin{equation}
	\bG=\big[\,\bD_\chi,\ \bA_\star,\ j\bA_\star\,\big],
	\label{eq:s_efim_jac}
\end{equation}
so the nuisance columns span, over the reals, the set
$\{\bA_\star\bv_1+j\bA_\star\bv_2:\bv_1,\bv_2\in\R^{2K}\}
=\coll(\bA_\star)$ regarded as a real subspace. That subspace is closed
under multiplication by $j$, and for $\ba,\bb\in\C^{M}$ the real inner
product $\Real\{\ba^{\mathsf H}\bb\}$ restricted to it therefore has the
same orthogonal projector as the complex one. Eliminating the nuisance block
by its Schur complement is exactly orthogonal projection of the geometric
columns onto the orthogonal complement of that subspace, which is
$\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}=\bI-\bA_\star\bA_\star^{\dagger}$. Hence
\begin{equation}
	\bJ_\chi^{\rm free}
	=\frac{2}{\sigma^2}\Real\{\bD_\chi^{\mathsf H}
	\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\},
	\label{eq:s_efim}
\end{equation}
which is the expression used in the main paper. Positive definiteness is
now immediate: for $\bu\in\R^4$,
\begin{equation}
	\bu^{\mathsf T}\bJ_\chi^{\rm free}\bu
	=\frac{2}{\sigma^2}\big\|\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\bu\big\|_2^2
	=\frac{2}{\sigma^2}\big\|\widetilde{\bD}_\chi\bu\big\|_2^2 ,
	\label{eq:s_efim_quad}
\end{equation}
so $\bJ_\chi^{\rm free}\succ\mathbf0$ if and only if $\widetilde{\bD}_\chi$
of \eqref{eq:s_ident} has trivial real nullspace, that is
$\rank_{\R}\widetilde{\bD}_\chi=4$. Since $\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}$ is built from the
Moore--Penrose inverse, \eqref{eq:s_efim} is the generalized Schur complement
of the nuisance block and is defined for any $\bA_\star$; it coincides with the
ordinary Schur complement exactly when $\bA_\star$ has full column rank, so
that the nuisance information block is invertible.

The statement is local and conditional. In particular, three panels are
not sufficient by equation counting alone: the RIS codes must preserve the
near-field spatial tangents, the curvature must be nondegenerate, and the
panel geometry must avoid collinearity and symmetry. Global permutations
and finite-bandwidth ambiguities are not excluded.

\section{Exact Complex-Scale Elimination}
\label{sec:technical_proofs_supp}

\begin{proposition}[Exact complex-scale elimination]
For every $\bp$ with $\bh_k(\bp)\ne\mathbf0$,
\begin{equation}
 \min_{\alpha_k\in\C}\|\tilde\bt_k-\alpha_k\bh_k(\bp)\|^2
 =\|\bPi_{\bh_k(\bp)}^\perp\tilde\bt_k\|^2,
 \quad
 \hat\alpha_k=\frac{\bh_k^{\mathsf H}\tilde\bt_k}{\|\bh_k\|^2}.
 \label{eq:s_scale}
\end{equation}
The profiled term is invariant to every nonzero complex rescaling of
$\bh_k$ and every phase rotation of $\tilde\bt_k$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{IEEEproof}
For fixed $\bp$, the objective is a strictly convex quadratic in the real
and imaginary parts of $\alpha_k$. Setting the Wirtinger derivative with
respect to $\alpha_k^\ast$ to zero yields \eqref{eq:s_scale}; substitution
gives the orthogonal residual. An orthogonal projector depends only on the
one-dimensional span of $\bh_k$, and its output norm is unchanged by a
phase rotation of the input.
\end{IEEEproof}

\section{Nested-Component Information Order}
\label{sec:split_proof_supp}

Theorem~\ref{main-thm:split} and its proof, including the Kronecker
projector splitting lemma on which both parts rest, are in the main paper.
A numerical audit and two remarks extend them.

The single-panel scalar identity follows from the calibrated
$\partial\bTheta/\partial\bchi=0$ model. The receiver-mode plots evaluate
the full multi-panel estimator, for which the additional qualifications
below are needed.

First, the exact single-panel ratio
$\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}=\|\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta\bx\|^2/\|\bTheta\bx\|^2$
depends on $k$ once several panels are resolved, so the weighted sum over
panels is in general not a global rescaling of the aggregate EFIM. The main
paper states the block-orthogonal approximation this admits; nothing beyond
the single-panel identity is claimed exactly.

Second, for \emph{nested} component sets the free-Jones information order
follows without any assumption on the Jones block, from the variational
identity
\begin{equation}
 \bu^{\mathsf T}\bJ_\chi^{\rm free}\bu
 =\frac{2}{\sigma^2}\min_{\delta\bx}
 \|\bD_\chi\bu+\bA\delta\bx\|^2 .
\end{equation}
Observing additional components appends nonnegative residual terms to the
minimized quadratic form, which therefore cannot decrease. This is why the
${\rm PEB}_{\rm scalar}\ge{\rm PEB}_{\rm dual}\ge{\rm PEB}_{\rm full}$
ordering of the reported bounds holds
even where a mask makes the retained Jones block singular and the exact
ratio $\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}$ is not by itself enough to order the
aggregate.

\section{Scope of the Three Projection Roles}
\label{sec:c2scope_supp}

The Maxwell--Kronecker projection plays three roles that the main paper
distinguishes but does not separate at length. Sufficiency and EFIM
preservation apply to the matched statistical model and require the
white-noise model of the main paper. The coupled recovery is equivariant only
\emph{after} the observation is projected, so it is the raw recovery applied
to a projected observation and can change the estimate through noise
suppression. Only the likelihood isometry is deterministic, holding pointwise
for every candidate and any observation, so it can move neither the final
objective nor its optimum. Pole preservation adds four conditions, namely
distinct poles, an adequate frequency aperture, nonzero paths, and full
compressed snapshot rank, which are recorded per trial rather than
assumed. Under colored noise an optimal processor would whiten or use a
covariance-weighted projector, so the unwhitened sweep of the main paper is a
mismatch test and not an extension of Theorem~\ref{main-thm:compress}.

\section{Generality and Degenerate Cases of Compression}
\label{sec:compress_proof_supp}

Theorem~\ref{main-thm:compress} is proved in the main paper. Two points
about its reach are recorded here.

The EFIM step uses only semi-unitarity of $\bU_S$, so the same argument
proves preservation under \emph{any} semi-unitary map whose range contains
both $\coll(\bA)$ and $\coll(\bD_\chi)$; the specific aggregate
Maxwell--Kronecker basis is one convenient such map, not the only one, and
it is the one that can be built without any UE-dependent quantity.

The generic-rank claim of part~(iv) is a measure-zero statement and
excludes named structural degeneracies rather than all of them. Each
$K\times K$ minor of the compressed non-frequency snapshot matrix is real
analytic in the continuously distributed training phases, Jones states and
nonzero gains, so a single feasible design with a nonzero minor implies
that this minor is not identically zero and its zero set is null. What this
does not cover, and what is therefore recorded per trial instead of
assumed, is $rT<K$, an annihilated path, a zero gain, or
systematically dependent panel signatures.

\section*{Part II: Numerical and Comparison Specifications}

\section{Reference Bounds and Additional Settings}
\label{sec:impl_supp}

This section gives the reference bounds and the numerical settings the
algorithm needs but the main text states only by name. All settings are
fixed across SNRs and trials unless they constitute the variable of a
stated ablation.

Table~\ref{tab:s_params} lists the settings not already fixed by main-paper
Table~I. At the default UE position the minimum path-delay separation is
$3.28$~ns, marginally above $1/B_{\rm occ}=3.23$~ns, and the clock search
interval occupies $5\%$ of the $1/\Delta f=200$~ns unambiguous period.

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Numerical settings additional to main-paper Table~I.}
\label{tab:s_params}
\centering
\scriptsize
\begin{tabular}{@{}l@{\quad}p{0.60\columnwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Setting & Value\\
\midrule
SNR definition & $10\log_{10}(\|\bmu_{\rm act}\|_2^2/(M_{\rm act}\sigma^2))$;
the receiver-mode study reuses the full-EVS $\sigma^2$ across masks\\
Basin diagnostic & Phase~I returns finite $\hat\bp_{\rm I}$ with
$\|\hat\bp_{\rm I}-\bp^\star\|_2\le0.1$~m\\
Path ranges & UE--RIS $3.63$--$4.17$~m; RIS--BS $28.63$--$31.24$~m\\
Support & known $K$; one cascaded path per panel; blocked LoS\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

In Table~\ref{tab:s_params}, $\bmu_{\rm act}$ stacks the noiseless samples
on the active receive components and $M_{\rm act}$ is their scalar count.
The noise variance is chosen separately for each trial; absolute path loss
is therefore absorbed into the SNR normalization.

Over the same $960$ draws per SNR as the benchmark, the root mean square of
the per-trial free-Jones PEB is $1.396$, $0.441$, $0.140$ and
$0.00441$~mm at $-20$, $-10$, $0$ and $30$~dB. Across all $10\,560$
benchmark draws the known-Jones-direction/free-Jones PEB ratio lies in
$[0.999960,1]$, and the projector and real-Schur calculations agree to
median relative deviation $4.15\times10^{-13}$ and maximum
$2.38\times10^{-12}$. These are numerical checks of the bound calculation,
not efficiency guarantees for the penalized estimator.

At $-10$~dB, $20$ independent training-code, Jones-state and gain
realizations at the reference geometry and default UE position give a median
free-Jones CEB of $3.3789$~ps, with range $3.2267$--$3.9675$~ps. The clock
bound plotted in Fig.~\ref{main-fig:benchmark_combined} is anchored at that
median and scaled by the free-Jones PEB. Like the position bound, it is a
local-information reference at the matched geometry and is not a lower bound
on a median error.

\subsection{Beam-Space Acquisition}

The adjoint pass forms
$\tilde\bg_k=\ba_{{\rm RB},k}^{\ast}\odot(\bOmega_k^{\mathsf H}\tilde\bt_k)$
and never inverts $\bOmega_k$. Trial ranges are $G_r=9$ points spaced
uniformly over the panel range bounds. At each of them the Fresnel dechirp
$e^{+j\kappa\|\brho_{k,m}\|^2/(2r)}$ is applied and a two-dimensional
transform zero-padded by a factor $4$ per axis, capped at $512$ bins per
axis, evaluates the correlation on the direction-cosine grid
$u=\lambda_c\nu_x/d_x$, $v=\lambda_c\nu_y/d_y$ at the transform frequencies
$\nu$. A bin is admissible when $u^2+v^2<1$ and when its normal cosine
$w=\sqrt{1-u^2-v^2}$ and azimuth fall inside the panel search bounds; the
phase-only response is front/back ambiguous, so only $w\ge0$ is enumerated.

Peaks are then taken on the range-maximized angular map
$\max_r S_r(u,v)$ rather than on the full cube, because the aperture
resolves direction far better than range. Four peaks are extracted greedily,
each followed by a guard of $\lceil 4/2\rceil=2$ bins per axis to prevent
re-selecting the same mainlobe, and every selected direction receives an
exact spherical range scan on $41$ uniformly spaced ranges before local
refinement. The retained fixed-count dictionary
$\mathcal C_k^{\rm cnt}$ is the $15\times9\times25$ range/elevation/azimuth
grid over the global panel bounds, and the local refinement grid is
$3\times3\times5$ in the same order over spans of $0.45$~m and $0.12$~rad
about the current iterate.

\subsection{Association}

The first tier evaluates all $K!$ permutations with the RIS term of
\eqref{main-eq:assoc_score} taken on the cached coarse codebook rather than
on the exact spherical projection, which is what makes the exhaustive
ranking affordable. The two permutations with the smallest coarse costs are
then re-evaluated using the exact spherical projection. The margin
$m_{\rm assoc}$ of main-paper Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:assoc_two_tier} is formed
from those two exact totals, and a trial is flagged unreliable when it falls
below $0.3$.

\subsection{Phase~II Solvers and Tolerances}

The inner clock search seeds its incumbent from a zero-padded transform of
length $M_c=4096$ over $\mathcal T$, then runs interval branch-and-bound
with the second-derivative bound \eqref{main-eq:bound_short}, bisecting the
interval of largest upper bound. Subdivision stops when the global gap falls
below
\begin{equation}
	\epsilon_{\rm clk}=M\,\epsilon_{\rm abs}
	+\epsilon_{\rm rel}\max\{\|\by\|^2,|Q_{\rm best}|\},
	\label{eq:eps_clk_supp}
\end{equation}
with $\epsilon_{\rm abs}=10^{-12}$ and $\epsilon_{\rm rel}=10^{-10}$, or
when the cap $N_{\rm cap}=2\times10^4$ subdivisions is reached; the reported
gap remains a valid bound in either case. The outer position search is
L-BFGS-B restricted to $\mathcal P$, with at most
$20$ iterations, relative objective tolerance $10^{-12}$ and
projected-gradient tolerance $10^{-8}$, and a
Powell restart, capped at $40$ iterations, when a near-tied clock branch makes
the envelope derivative unreliable; a branch is declared near-tied when the
competing objectives differ by less than $10^{-10}$ in absolute value or
$10^{-8}$ in relative value. The common-geometry stage runs
L-BFGS-B from its four deterministic starts under its own settings: at most
$30$ iterations, relative objective tolerance $10^{-10}$ and
projected-gradient tolerance $10^{-6}$.
Those four starts are built in a fixed order and contain no random or oracle
state. The first is the clipped coordinate-wise median of the $K$ panel-local
fixes, $\bp_0=\mathrm{clip}_{\mathcal P}\{\mathrm{median}_k
\hat\bp_k^{\rm loc}\}$. The remaining budget is filled by taking at most one
of the axis-aligned displacements of $\bp_0$ by $\pm25$~cm, then the height
samples that replace the third coordinate of $\bp_0$ by a $7$-point grid
spanning $\mathcal P$ ordered outward from its center, and only then the
remaining axial displacements;
every candidate is clipped back to $\mathcal P$ and duplicates are dropped,
so height diversity is reached before the axial sweep is exhausted. Among the
resulting candidates, those whose clock replicas
$\hat\tau_k-(\|\bp-\br_k\|+d_{{\rm RB},k})/c_0$ have standard deviation at most
$0.5$~ns are retained when any exist; of these, the ones within a relative
$10^{-3}$ of the best objective are near-optimal, and $\hat\bp_{\rm GI}$ is the
one among them with the smallest clock spread.

The per-column confidence weights of main-paper
Eq.~\eqref{main-eq:Jgeom_short} use $\varrho_0=1/2$ and
$\varpi_{\min}=0.05$, so
a column whose rank-one split is poor is down-weighted smoothly while every
panel stays in the fit; $\varpi_{\min}>0$ is what prevents a single bad split
from removing a panel outright. The refreshed Jones anchor of
\eqref{main-eq:refresh_short} is
$\hat\be_k=\hat\bx_{{\rm ref},k}/\|\hat\bx_{{\rm ref},k}\|_2$ whenever
$\|\hat\bx_{{\rm ref},k}\|_2>10^{-12}$, which holds throughout the reported
experiments, and the Jones direction already carried by the Phase-I estimate
otherwise. Either way $\|\hat\be_k\|_2=1$, so $\bPi_{\hat\be_k}^{\perp}$ is a
rank-one orthogonal projector and the penalty weight of
\eqref{main-eq:penalized_short} is the same in both cases.

\subsection{Coupled-Recovery Loading}

The coupled recovery \eqref{main-eq:coupledls_short} uses the Tikhonov
loading $\lambda_\tau=\lambda_{\rm rel}
\tr(\bD_{\rm lift}^{\mathsf T}\bD_{\rm lift}^{\ast})/K+\lambda_{\rm fl}$,
where $\bD_{\rm lift}$ collects the $K$ lifted design columns above, with the
dimensionless relative term $\lambda_{\rm rel}=10^{-6}$ normalizing by that
design Gram and the absolute floor $\lambda_{\rm fl}=10^{-12}$. Neither
constant enters the reconstruction of the two frequency factors from the
shared pole, so neither can move a delay estimate.

\section{Baseline Definitions and Adaptations}
\label{sec:baselinespec_supp}

All methods use the same observations and trial realizations within each
experimental cell. Table~\ref{tab:s_baselinespec} specifies their acquisition
models, read-out rules, numerical settings, and adaptations to the
blocked-LoS, polarimetric common-clock setting. All settings are fixed across
SNRs and trials, and none is tuned per method.

Each route is an \emph{adaptation} to the common forward model, not the
reference algorithm run unmodified: none of the three references addresses a
blocked-LoS, polarimetric, common-clock geometry with $K$ oriented near-field
panels, and the adaptation row names what changed. Each is stopped at the
method-specific read-out or refinement its own cited formulation prescribes,
so the residual gap measures the reliability of that formulation's own
acquisition and read-out on this scene; Section~\ref{sec:aspub} reports
separately what changes when the same exact-model refinement is applied to
every output. Undefined estimates remain in the denominator and are
classified as catastrophic failures.

