\section*{Part III: Additional Numerical Results}

\section{Compression Diagnostics}
\label{sec:compdiag_supp}

The aggregate Maxwell subspace attains its predicted dimension in every
trial of the operating region: the numerical rank of $\bPsi$ is $r=2K=6$
throughout, so the receive dimension falls from $I=96$ to $6$ with no
observed rank deficiency. The retained energy fraction rises from the
white-noise floor $r/I=1/16$, which is what a signal-free observation
projects onto a $6$-of-$96$ subspace, to $0.9991$ at $30$~dB, so the
projection discards essentially only noise once the signal dominates.

The subspace-leakage ratio $\sigma_{K+1}/\sigma_K$ measures how cleanly the
retained delay subspace separates from the noise subspace, smaller being
better. At $-10$~dB it falls from $0.824$ in the raw EVS mode to $0.357$
after compression, which is the margin that produces the p95 delay-error
improvement quoted in Section~\ref{main-subsec:res_c2}.

Below the operating region, the single-factor protocol changes only the
observation used for delay subspace estimation; both arms use compression
downstream.
At $-15$~dB, compression lowers p95 Phase-I delay error from $29.0$ to
$1.93$~ns, raises basin acquisition from $93.54\%$ to $97.92\%$, and
lowers $P_{\rm cat}$ from $6.04\%$ to $1.67\%$.
At $-20$~dB, the corresponding changes are $46.2$ to $32.2$~ns,
$80.42\%$ to $93.54\%$, and $17.92\%$ to $4.79\%$.
The $-10$-dB p95 reduction is $3.96$ to $0.156$~ns.
The projection therefore improves this finite-sample delay estimator,
but does not prevent subspace collapse: at $-20$~dB, compressed leakage
is already $0.811$, versus $0.995$ without compression.

\section{Receiver Modes}
\label{sec:recvmodes_supp}

This section reports the retained-polarization weight that
Theorem~\ref{main-thm:split}(i) predicts and the end-to-end receiver ordering
over the full tested SNR range.

The weight $\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}=\|\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_k\bx_k\|^2/
\|\bTheta_k\bx_k\|^2$ of Theorem~\ref{main-thm:split}(i) quoted in the main
paper is computed directly, without synthesizing an observation or running an
estimator. It is a noiseless per-panel ratio in which the complex gain
$\beta_k$ scales numerator and denominator alike and cancels, and the
resolvability sweep fixes the Jones states of the two controlled paths
analytically from the overlap: $\gamma_{\rm ref}=\pi/2$ and
$\gamma_{\rm cmp}=\arcsin(\text{overlap})$ at $\eta=0$. Only $\bTheta_k$, the
two Jones vectors and the receiver mask are therefore needed. The separation
sweep moves the panels, so the geometry is rebuilt at every separation rather
than evaluated once at the default one.

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Retained-polarization weight $\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}$ for the
reference and competing panels of the controlled pair, median over $48$
draws at $1$~ns separation.}
\label{tab:s_alpha}
\centering
\scriptsize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{2}{c}{Scalar} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Dual-pol.}
& \multicolumn{2}{c}{Full EVS}\\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3}\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\cmidrule(lr){6-7}
Overlap & ref. & comp. & ref. & comp. & ref. & comp.\\
\midrule
$0.1$ & $0.110$ & $0.002$ & $0.500$ & $0.005$ & $1.000$ & $1.000$\\
$0.5$ & $0.110$ & $0.026$ & $0.500$ & $0.125$ & $1.000$ & $1.000$\\
$0.9$ & $0.110$ & $0.074$ & $0.500$ & $0.405$ & $1.000$ & $1.000$\\
$1.0$ & $0.110$ & $0.087$ & $0.500$ & $0.500$ & $1.000$ & $1.000$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:s_alpha} is the source of the two dual-mask values quoted in
the main text. The weight is not a single number per mode: it is $0.500$ for
the reference panel at every overlap and falls to $0.005$ for the competing
panel at overlap $0.1$, which is the panel dependence
Theorem~\ref{main-thm:split}(i) predicts once a mask retains fewer than the
full six components. Full EVS holds unity for both panels throughout, so no
polarization energy is lost there by construction.

Across the delay-separation axis the dual and full weights are constant to
the reported precision, so the panel dependence above is an overlap effect
and not a by-product of the panels moving; the scalar reference weight drifts
from $0.111$ to $0.065$ between $0.1$ and $5$~ns separation. These values
quantify retained energy only, and they are not a control that separates
retained energy from panel separability in the resolution sweep.

The end-to-end ordering persists below the operating region. At $-15$~dB
the scalar, dual-polarized and
full-EVS catastrophic rates are $43.75\%$, $10.63\%$ and $1.88\%$; at
$-20$~dB they are $85.83\%$, $31.67\%$ and $5.00\%$. Only $14.17\%$ of
scalar trials remain position inliers at $-20$~dB, so the reduced-mode
conditional efficiencies there rest on few trials.

\section{Calibration and Model Mismatch}
\label{sec:mismatch_supp}

Fig.~\ref{fig:s_robustness} gives the four mismatch sweeps at $-10$~dB with
generation-side perturbation only; panel~(a), the binding RIS--BS
incidence-angle boundary, is discussed in the main paper and is repeated
here for context.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_robustness.pdf}
\caption{Robustness boundaries, $480$ paired trials per point, with
generation-side mismatch only; (a)--(c) are measured at $-10$~dB and the
model-order sweep of~(d) at $0$~dB. (a)~RIS--BS incidence-angle error:
solid filled and dashed hollow curves are catastrophic and basin-acquisition
rates. (b)~Receiver-side perturbations against sweep-normalized mismatch;
the physical maxima are stated in the legend. (c)~Unwhitened colored-noise
boundary. (d)~Discrete model-order mismatch; the median and p95 on the
right-hand axis diverge by four orders of magnitude under overestimation.}
\label{fig:s_robustness}
\end{figure*}

Each perturbation is drawn independently per trial. EVS phase errors are
zero-mean Gaussian per panel and component; EVS gains are log-normal,
$\exp(\sigma Z-\sigma^2/2)$ with $Z$ standard normal, so the swept level
$\sigma$ is a log-gain standard deviation and the gain has unit mean; the
RIS--BS direction error is an isotropic Gaussian in the tangent plane of the
nominal unit direction, renormalized; BS sensor offsets are Gaussian and
independent per coordinate. The colored-noise sweep draws circularly
symmetric noise with $[\bR]_{ij}=\rho^{|i-j|}$ across the EVS index, white
across tones and training, rescaled to the target SNR on the active
components.

\emph{Receiver-side calibration.} Table~\ref{tab:s_calib} compares the
tested receiver perturbations with the RIS--BS angular boundary. Across
EVS log-gain standard deviations up to $0.1$, phase errors up to $10^\circ$,
and BS sensor-position errors up to $0.5$~mm, proposed catastrophic rates
remain between $0.21\%$ and $0.62\%$. Conditional RMSE increases by at most
$20\%$, from $0.416$ to $0.497$~mm, while reconstruction NMSE changes much
more. By comparison, a $0.25^\circ$ RIS--BS angular error raises conditional
RMSE to $31.1$~mm. These results describe the tested mismatch ranges and
do not extend the matched-model sufficiency theorem.

