\emph{RIS element-phase calibration} (Fig.~\ref{fig:s_ris_calibration})
perturbs individual RIS phases at $0$~dB. The Phase-I-only median grows
from $6.58$~mm at zero error to $39.6$~mm at $10^\circ$ and $77.4$~mm at
$20^\circ$, where its catastrophic rate is $31.67\%$. The refined route's
curves show the corresponding improvement on the shared observations, and the
oracle-calibrated median PEB remains near $0.124$~mm.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_ris_calibration.pdf}
\caption{RIS element-phase calibration at $0$~dB, $480$ paired trials
per level. Filled and hollow markers denote median and p95 position errors,
respectively, for the refined estimator and the Phase-I-only output.}
\label{fig:s_ris_calibration}
\end{figure}

\emph{Colored noise.} The objective is unwhitened. At correlation
$\rho=0,0.5,0.8$, proposed catastrophic rates are $0.83\%,0.42\%,0.21\%$,
compared with $1.04\%,1.88\%,2.71\%$ for the raw-delay control.
Proposed conditional RMSE changes from $0.436$ to $0.310$~mm across
the sweep. Sufficiency under colored noise would require whitening, which is
not applied here.

\emph{Model order.} At $0$~dB, $\widehat K=2$ gives a $0.705$-m median
and $65.63\%$ catastrophic events. For $\widehat K=4$ and $5$, medians
remain $0.150$ and $0.299$~mm but catastrophic rates are $34.79\%$ and
$47.92\%$, with p95 errors $1.27$ and $1.73$~m. Thus small medians under
overestimation coexist with a large failure population. The model assumes
known panel count; these results do not support robust order selection.

Section~\ref{sec:squint_supp} records that every reported experiment generates
and estimates on the same center-frequency spatial manifold, so the neglected
frequency dependence contributes exactly zero residual there. This section
measures it directly instead. The observation is generated with a
per-subcarrier manifold, $f_n=f_c+\nu_n\Delta f$ and $\lambda_n=c_0/f_n$ at
the centered tone indices, and processed by the unchanged center-frequency
estimator. Panel geometry, element coordinates and the physical ULA spacing
are fixed across tones; the RIS codes, Jones states and gains are one draw
shared by all tones; and the center-path baseband delay phase is unchanged, so
the spatial responses add only element-relative path differences. In the
reference configuration the mismatch carries $0.13\%$ of the signal energy,
of which $7.5$~parts per million leave the calibrated receive subspace.

Two estimates are reported per sample. The first-order prediction solves
$\min_{\Delta\bz\in\R^4}\|\bP\boldsymbol{\delta}-\bP\bD_{\bz}\Delta\bz\|^2$ in the real
parameters, with $\boldsymbol{\delta}=[\bA_{\rm wb}-\bA_c]\bx^\star$,
$\bP=\bI-\bA_c\bA_c^{+}$, $\bz=[\bp^{\mathsf T},c_0\Delta t]^{\mathsf T}$ and
$\bx^\star$ constructed from the generator rather than fitted. The nonlinear
value is a noiseless local refinement from the true position. Both are free of
the directional penalty; a run with the paper penalty changes them by at most
$0.6\%$.

