The linearization is accurate at every sample, the two columns agreeing to
between $9\times10^{-5}$ and $2\times10^{-2}$ in relative terms, so no
higher-order check is needed. The zero-mismatch controls return the true
parameters to $2.6\times10^{-12}$~m and $2\times10^{-11}$~ps, nine orders below
the tightest accuracy scale compared against, so the reported bias is the
wideband mismatch and not a solver residual.

Position and clock behave differently. The position bias spans
0.0037--0.0804~mm, median 0.0186~mm, and stays at or below $0.18$ of the
sample's own PEB throughout, so it is not visible against the noise at the
low end of the evaluated range. The clock bias spans 0.19--5.38~ps, median
1.93~ps, and reaches $1.6$ times the sample's own CEB, exceeding it at four
of the nine samples. Because the bias is deterministic while the bounds scale
with $\sigma$, both ratios grow as the SNR rises.

This is a local refinement diagnostic: it does not exercise Phase-I
acquisition, and nothing here bounds the catastrophic rate or the basin
behavior under spatial wideband.

\section{External Benchmark}
\label{sec:benchmark_full_supp}

The main paper reports the benchmark over ${\rm SNR}\in[-10,30]$~dB. This
section adds the two points below that range. Every route is evaluated at
its method-specific read-out, so the separation reflects each baseline's own
acquisition and read-out rather than a shared final fit; position quantiles
are nearest-rank order statistics.

The ordering is unchanged below the operating region. Among the evaluated
adaptations the proposed route has the lowest median, p95 and catastrophic
position rate at both lower SNRs (Table~\ref{tab:s_benchmark}).
At $-15$ and $-20$~dB its catastrophic rates are $1.35\%$ and $3.44\%$,
versus $6.25\%$ and $22.40\%$ for baseline-initialized 4D-JVP,
$60.63\%$ and $78.33\%$ for algebraic CPD,
$18.13\%$ and $31.15\%$ for VBI/SBL, and $94.79\%$ and $96.35\%$
for the evaluated OMP adaptation.
The latter includes a far-field direction-acquisition stage in a
deep-near-field scene. These results characterize this adaptation and
scene, not the general low-SNR capability of grid-based estimation.

Clock catastrophic rates at the same SNRs are $1.15\%$ and $2.71\%$
for the proposed route, versus $4.69\%$ and $17.71\%$ for 4D-JVP.
The native CPD, VBI/SBL and OMP rates are respectively
$15.31/38.13\%$, $14.06/24.17\%$ and $76.35/79.90\%$.

