\section{Effect of a Common Exact-Model Refinement}
\label{sec:aspub}

The primary benchmark evaluates each adapted baseline using its
method-specific read-out. To distinguish acquisition error from read-out
error, an auxiliary comparison appends the same bounded exact-model
position--clock refinement to the outputs of VBI/SBL and Algebraic CPD. The
auxiliary trials are independent of the primary benchmark; therefore only
comparisons within each protocol are paired. Near-field OMP already carries
its prescribed SAGE refinement and is unchanged by this comparison.

At $-10$~dB, VBI/SBL's median changes from $26.1$~mm under its
method-specific read-out to $0.322$~mm under the common refinement, with
catastrophic rates $9.90\%$ and $0.83\%$. Under the common refinement the
proposed route has $0.320$~mm median and $0.42\%$ catastrophic events. The
paired comparison shows eight discordant catastrophic events in one
direction and four in the other ($p=0.388$, McNemar exact), so a rate
difference is not established there. For this blocked-direct-path
adaptation of~\cite{li2024vbi}, the gap seen in the primary benchmark is
therefore consistent with read-out rather than acquisition error.

Algebraic CPD's median under the common refinement is $0.664$~mm, but its
catastrophic rate remains $42.92\%$ ($46.67\%$ under its method-specific
read-out). Against the proposed route the discordant catastrophic events are
$408$ to zero, so this adaptation retains a catastrophic-rate floor that the
added refinement does not remove.

