Below the operating region the degradation is concentrated in the
delay-error tail. Median compressed subspace leakage
$\sigma_{K+1}/\sigma_K$ rises from $0.357$ at $-10$~dB to $0.574$ and
$0.811$ at $-15$ and $-20$~dB, and the p95 Phase-I delay error grows from
$0.110$ to $0.711$ and $21.9$~ns against medians of $0.025$, $0.049$ and
$0.130$~ns. The proposed catastrophic rate over the same points rises from
$0.21\%$ to $1.25\%$ and $5.63\%$, and basin acquisition falls from
$99.79\%$ to $98.75\%$ and $93.75\%$. These diagnostics indicate weakening
delay-subspace separation; they do not establish that every final error
originates in Phase~I.

\section{Phase II Directional-Weight Ablation}
\label{sec:lambda_supp}

The main paper reads the free-Jones PEB as a reference for the unpenalized
data model and does not claim that the penalized estimator attains it. This
section measures the finite-SNR effect of the fixed $\lambda_k=1$ using the
full-EVS arms of a dedicated weight sweep: four weights
$\{0,0.1,1,10\}$, five SNRs and $480$ shared-initializer trials per cell,
or $9\,600$ runs. No run fails. Each arm uses the same noisy observation
and Phase-I output; $\lambda_k=0$ removes the directional penalty, with
the common numerical loading retained.

Table~\ref{tab:s_lambda} reports conditional position and clock RMSE,
each conditioned on its own catastrophic-event complement as in the main
paper. The largest change over the weight range is
$6.23\times10^{-6}$~mm in position and $3.76\times10^{-5}$~ps in clock.
All weights produce the same catastrophic indicators in this full-EVS
sweep. The largest penalty/data-objective ratio is $6.45\times10^{-6}$.
Thus the penalty has little measured effect on these full-EVS results.

